---
import ServicePage from "../components/ServicePage.astro";

const specs = [
  {
    name: "Maximum height of the work piece",
    value: "420 mm",
    note: "Makino with H.E.A.T technology handles our tallest, heaviest wire-cutting jobs",
  },
  {
    name: "Positional tolerance",
    value: "±0.003 mm",
    note: "typical for finish passes on stable material",
  },
  {
    name: "Taper cutting",
    value: "4-axis",
    note: "programmed tapers, sharp internal corners and fine detail",
  },
  {
    name: "Wire size",
    value: "0.1–0.3 mm",
    note: "wire diameter selected to suit the profile and finish",
  },
  {
    name: "Surface roughness",
    value: "2 µm",
    note: "with multiple skim passes",
  },
  {
    name: "Edge condition",
    value: "burr-free",
    note: "spark-eroded edges need no deburring pass",
  },
  {
    name: "Cutting force",
    value: "0 N",
    note: "non-contact erosion. Thin walls and delicate features stay flat",
  },
  {
    name: "Heat-affected zone",
    value: "minimal",
    note: "skim passes remove the recast layer where the job demands it",
  },
  {
    name: "Materials",
    value: "any conductive metal",
    note: "tool steel, stainless, titanium, carbide, copper, exotic alloys (and many more)",
  },
];

const applications = [
  {
    name: "Press tools & dies",
    body: "Punches, dies and die sections cut after heat treatment, so the profile you receive is the profile that runs. Matched punch-and-die sets from the same program guarantee clearance.",
  },
  {
    name: "Hardened dies & die sections",
    body: "Cutting features into dies after heat treatment, so nothing distorts afterwards. Apertures, relief and repairs in material a cutter can't touch.",
  },
  {
    name: "Splines, keyways & internal forms",
    body: "Forms a broach can't justify and a mill can't reach: internal splines, hexes, keyways and irregular apertures, started from a wire-threading hole anywhere in the part.",
  },
  {
    name: "Slitting & segmenting hardened stock",
    body: "Clean, square, burr-free separation of hardened and delicate material where an abrasive cut-off would burn, burr or bend the part.",
  },
  {
    name: "Medical & instrumentation components",
    body: "Burr-free edges and no mechanical stress on small stainless and titanium parts. The wire never pushes on the work, so 0.3 mm walls come off the machine straight.",
  },
  {
    name: "Repair, modification & seized assemblies",
    body: "Opening a hardened bore, adding a keyway to a finished shaft, or eroding through a seized joint without force, so the component that must survive does survive.",
  },
];
---

<ServicePage
  title="EDM Wire Cutting Solution, Sydney | Wire EDM Pro"
  description="Wire EDM cutting in Sydney: ±0.003 mm tolerances, hardened steel to 60+ HRC, burr-free profiles in any conductive metal. Quotes from drawings or STEP files."
  heroKicker="EDM Wire Cutting Solution"
  heroTitle="The wire never touches the work."
  heroImage="/images/fanuc-c600ia"
  heroImageAlt="Fanuc RoboCut C600 wire EDM machine at Wire EDM Pro, Sydney"
  serviceName="EDM Wire Cutting"
  relatedIndustries={["tool-die-making", "aerospace-defence", "medical"]}
  heroLede="Wire EDM erodes metal with a spark, not a cutting edge. A charged wire traces the programmed profile while dielectric water flushes the gap, reaching what conventional tooling can't: hardened dies, splines, keyways and fine detail, cut after heat treatment. On our Makino and Fanuc RoboCut machines, hardened steel cuts as readily as aluminium, and the part feels no force at all."
  specs={specs}
  specNote="Figures are typical wire EDM process capability; tolerance and finish for your part depend on geometry, material and thickness. We confirm both at quote."
  applications={applications}
  ctaHeading="Hardened, thin or awkward?"
  ctaBody="Those are the jobs wire EDM was built for. Send the drawing and we'll tell you if there's a cheaper way. Or if this is the only way."
>
  <section slot="deep" class="edm-vs section">
    <div class="container edm-vs__grid">
      <div>
        <h2>When wire EDM beats the mill</h2>
        <p class="edm-vs__body">
          Milling and turning remove metal by force; EDM removes it by spark
          erosion. That difference decides which process your part wants. Cut <em
            >after</em
          > hardening and the profile can't move in heat treatment. Cut without contact
          and thin webs don't deflect. Cut with a wire and an inside corner needs
          only the wire's radius. Not a cutter's. And because the work sits submerged
          in a flushed dielectric bath, the surface finish stays consistent from the
          first millimetre to the last.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="edm-vs__list">
        <li>
          Capable of cutting all metals hardened, soft, ferrous, and
          non‑ferrous
        </li>
        <li>Inside corners tighter than any available cutter radius</li>
        <li>Walls or webs too thin to survive clamping and cutting force</li>
        <li>Burrs are unacceptable and hand-finishing is not an option</li>
        <li>Carbide or exotic alloys that destroy conventional tooling</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServicePage>

<style>
  .edm-vs {
    padding-block: var(--space-2xl);
  }
  .edm-vs__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 6fr) minmax(0, 5fr);
    gap: var(--space-2xl);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .edm-vs h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    max-width: 22ch;
  }
  .edm-vs__body {
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }
  .edm-vs__list {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }
  .edm-vs__list li {
    padding: var(--space-sm) 0 var(--space-sm) var(--space-lg);
    border-bottom: var(--rule-hairline);
    position: relative;
  }
  .edm-vs__list li::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 1.15em;
    width: 0.55rem;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--color-accent);
  }
  @media (max-width: 56rem) {
    .edm-vs__grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--space-lg);
    }
  }
</style>
